  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIncreased ridging and a sfc high building into the region out of \nCanada will result in mostly dry conditions for today through \nSaturday. Temperatures will cool a few degrees to the mid 70s to low \n80s out ahead of the Canadian sfc high. \n\nWhile mostly dry weather is expected, a few passing mid-level \ntroughs over or just south of the forecast area may cause showers at \na minimum, with a few thunderstorms also possible. Guidance is still \nsomewhat uncertain with timing or track of the passing troughs \nwithin the northwest flow of the large ridge still over the \nCentral portion of the CONUS. The first trough there is some \nbetter agreement that the trough will pass just south of the \nforecast area later Thursday evening into early Friday morning. \nAnother trough will track across the region on Friday night into\nSaturday morning, but guidance is still not in as much \nagreement with track and rain potential. Timing and track of the\npassing troughs will be important as the lack of daytime \nheating and displacement from better moisture will help to \nminimize rain potential and especially thunderstorm potential. \nThe best potential for showers/storms is expected for the Friday\nevening/night trough. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA potent shortwave trough and sfc low tracking across the northern \nfringe of the large ridge over the Central CONUS will move into the \narea on Sunday afternoon. While it is still too far out for the \nhigher resolution models, this setup within a northwest flow will \nresult in showers and thunderstorms at a minimum. As usual, timing \nand amount of instability available, including cloud cover, will \nfactor in significantly for the potential for this system to cause \nstrong to severe thunderstorms for Sunday. Trends will be monitored \nas more higher resolution guidance becomes available for Sunday \nafternoon. An influx of moisture ahead of the approaching sfc low \nwill also help increase the potential for heavier rain within \nshowers/storms.",
